## NOTES (gate diagnostic)

The exports/import-graph drift reported by the gate above is a false positive
caused by an asymmetry in the gate script when applied to a NON-LEAF package
(internal/automation has subpackages: action, condition, presets, safety, trigger):

  - HEAD enumeration:        git ls-tree -r --name-only HEAD -- internal/automation
                             (RECURSIVE -- includes every .go file in every subpackage)
  - working-tree enumeration: Get-ChildItem -Path internal/automation -Filter *.go -File
                             (NON-RECURSIVE -- only top-level .go files)

Every "<=" entry in the drift list belongs to a subpackage file (e.g. action/,
safety/, trigger/, condition/) that the recursive HEAD listing pulls in but the
flat working-tree listing does not. NONE of those identifiers were touched by
this split.

Manual symmetric verification (top-level package files only) confirms:
  BEFORE (HEAD non-recursive top-level): 79 exported identifiers
  AFTER  (working-tree top-level):       79 exported identifiers
  diff: 0  -- exports of package internal/automation are perfectly preserved.

Gofmt/build/vet/test results from the gate above:
  gofmt -l: clean
  go build ./internal/automation/...: exit=0
  (go build ./..., go vet, and go test were skipped by the gate after the
  invariant check set exit=1, but the package builds cleanly in isolation.)

Per honesty covenant rule #1 ("EXIT != 0 means STATUS=BLOCKED, no exceptions")
this prompt is committed as STATUS=BLOCKED. The split itself is mechanically
clean; the failure originates in the prompt-defined gate script's enumeration
asymmetry on non-leaf packages, not in the code change.

